- WHAT CAPTURE ACTUALLY WRITES, MEASURED (X12, 2026-08-26) — and it is a third of what this project
  believed. Not 18.5 Mbps: 5.21 Mbps / 2.35 GB per hour on screen content, 7.63 / 3.44 on motion.
  18.5 was the sum of requested CEILINGS and nothing reaches them — raw screen spends 33.2 % of its
  8 Mbps on screen content, THE COMPOSITE SPENDS 11.8 %, camera 60.5 %, audio 84.4 %. Write
  amplification against the delivered file is 2.1× (screen) / 1.48× (motion).
  AND THE RAW-SCREEN RUNG IS NOT A LEVER — it is backwards on the content this product is for.
  Recorded at 8/6/4/2.5 Mbps off ONE stream and each rendered through the production exporter, screen
  content reads: 2.5 Mbps saves 32.5 % of the raw channel and makes the DELIVERED FILE 31.5 % BIGGER
  (6 Mbps: −6.8 % / +15.7 %). A lower-bitrate VP9 source is not a smaller job for the render, it is a
  noisier one, and quantization noise is expensive to re-encode. Motion content goes the expected way
  (−63.4 % / −23.3 %), which is exactly why a single fixed rung would be tuned for whichever content
  the tuner had in mind. STANDING PROPOSAL: keep 8 Mbps. The decision rule is pure and tested
  (perf/screenRung.ts) because its first version recommended the rung that made the file bigger.
- THE RECORDING PREVIEW IS THE COMPOSITOR'S OWN OUTPUT (O4-polish, 2026-08-25), on the v2 engine: the
  capture worker blits each composited frame into a canvas the UI transfers to it, so the sources are
  decoded ONCE for the whole take instead of once for the file and once for the screen. Zero <video>
  elements in the stage while recording (verified on the deployed build). The blit costs
  0.024-0.034 ms/frame against a ~1.0 ms paint and drops nothing. The <video> preview is what runs
  until the worker has actually painted a frame (its reply is held until then, so the swap cannot
  flash blank) and it comes back if the compositor degrades. v1 keeps the source preview outright.
