- AND THE AUDIO LANES SHOW THEIR SOUND (F8, same day). Each audio lane carries a waveform over its own
  tint — one column per two device pixels, each an honest SAMPLE taken by random access rather than a
  summary of a full scan, so the cost follows the lane's width and not the take's length: 396-400
  columns in 31-186 ms per channel, against 255-545 ms for a filmstrip. It is normalised to a high
  PERCENTILE of its own columns, not to the maximum: a system-audio channel whose steady tone sits
  near 0.05 opened with a 2.2 transient, and against the max every other column drew at 2 % of the
  lane and the whole channel read as empty. That rule is pure and tested (compose/lanewave.ts
  waveScale). Both halves are one hook (app/hooks/useLaneArt.ts): video first, one decoder at a time,
  width-bucketed so a resize does not re-decode, and every failure silent and total.
- THE TIMELINE SHOWS THE TAKE, NOT A RECTANGLE (F8 filmstrip, 2026-08-25). Every VIDEO lane carries a
  strip of the channel's own frames; audio lanes are unchanged. Decoded by RANDOM ACCESS
  (mediabunny `samplesAtTimestamps`) rather than compose/video.ts's forward walk, which would decode
  54,000 frames for a strip over a 30-minute take, and stitched into ONE image so the timeline paints
  one background however long the take is. The layout rule is a PITCH (76 CSS px between thumbnail
  centres, at most 24), so length changes what each thumbnail STANDS FOR and never how many there
  are. Measured on the prod build in real Chrome: screen 11/11 frames in 493-958 ms, camera 11/11 in
  291-315 ms. Over a picture the colour language changes register — the kept span keeps its channel
  tint at 0.18 opacity so frames read through it, a cut span goes DARK rather than fainter — and the
  lane grows 24 → 30 px while .tl__lanes tightens its gap to 5 px, because .tl is a fixed 184 px and
  the lane list already overflowed at four channels. Every failure is silent and total: no strip, and
  the lane looks exactly as it did before. Nothing in the export imports it.
